BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:darc-transmediale-interstices-research-workshop-2027@artificialnouveau.
 github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T221005Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260828
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260829
SUMMARY:Deadline: transmediale x DARC: Interstices\, Research (in) the Gaps
 \, PhD/Research Workshop
DESCRIPTION:The annual DARC PhD/Research Workshop\, organised by the Digita
 l Aesthetics Research Center at Aarhus University with transmediale\, resp
 onds to transmediale's 2027 thematic framework. THEME: 'Interstices: Resea
 rch (in) the gaps'. An interstice is the small space between objects set c
 losely together\, the cords in a rope or the atoms in a crystal lattice: a
 n emptiness produced by the structure it belongs to\, and without which th
 at structure could not be what it is. In crystallography\, interstitial si
 tes are the spaces a lattice and its basis generate but do not fill\, and 
 interstitial diffusion\, the movement of small atoms through the gaps of a
  host lattice\, is the mechanism by which materials transform. The call as
 ks what the equivalent sites are in social\, cultural and computational st
 ructures: where are the gaps these combinations generate but do not fill\,
  what moves through them\, and do they carry transformative power? It work
 s through Yancey Strickler's dark forest theory of the internet (the withd
 rawal of meaningful exchange from the open\, surveilled\, indexed web into
  private channels)\, the Dark Forest Collective's anthology and its 'cozyw
 eb' of small sheltered spaces\, dark nets\, miniverses and moving castles 
 as interstitial spaces produced by and defined against the dominant web\, 
 and Bogna Konior's extension of the dark forest metaphor to the opacity of
  AI systems\, where silence is refusal rather than absence and the forest 
 is increasingly inhabited by other-than-human intelligences that opt out o
 f engagement with people. Where big tech pursues efficiency through the au
 tomation of human perception\, cognition\, identification and aesthetics\,
  artists and researchers explore the non/more-than-human\, glitchy\, noisy
 \, machinic\, datafied and dark to build new expression and critique of da
 ta-ism and what is marketed as artificial intelligence\, including the dan
 gers and potentials inside badly curated data models\, between polluting d
 ata centres\, and in skeuomorphic interfaces. WHAT THEY WANT: proposals fr
 om artists\, researchers and practitioners for work that occupies and move
 s through gaps that are social as well as structural\, ontological and mat
 erial. The gaps are physical too: the heat of data centres and their thirs
 t for water\, latency in the cables\, unindexed nodes. The call invites sp
 eculations\, practical interventions and inferences that fill the gaps and
  expand the diversity of possible communications and communities that can 
 inhabit dark forests\, including those built on\, against and beneath the 
 infrastructures underlying them\, and welcomes work on the (dis-)continuou
 s histories of technological exploration of computational infrastructures 
 over the last 30 years or longer\, and on how current artistic\, theoretic
 al and technical explorations repeat or break from them. WHO: PhD candidat
 es and early-career researchers\, with submissions welcome from researcher
 s inside and outside the academy\, including artists\, designers and curat
 ors. PARTICIPANT EXPECTATIONS: participants write a paper and present it a
 t the workshop\, and on the basis of that peer review shorten the research
  into a newspaper-length article for distribution at the transmediale fest
 ival. The workshop is led by scholars from DARC at Aarhus University. DEAD
 LINE: 28 August 2026\, 23:59 CEST\, via the application link on the call p
 age. Queries to Pablo Velasco\, pvelasco[at]cc.au.dk.\n\nOrganisation: Dig
 ital Aesthetics Research Center (DARC)\, Aarhus University\, in collaborat
 ion with transmediale\nLocation: Berlin\, Germany\; the workshop runs alon
 gside transmediale 2027 (festival 26 to 31 January 2027). Open to research
 ers based anywhere\, inside or outside the academy.\nAward: No submission 
 fee. The published call does not state a participation fee\, honorarium\, 
 travel or accommodation support\, so confirm with the organisers before bu
 dgeting. Accepted participants present a paper at the workshop and their c
 ondensed text is distributed as a newspaper-style article at the festival.
 \nDuration: Research workshop held around transmediale 2027 in Berlin (fes
 tival 26 to 31 January 2027)\n\nMore: https://transmediale.de/en/news/open
 -call-for-darc-phd-research-workshop-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugran
 tdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://transmediale.de/en/news/open-call-for-darc-phd-research-worksho
 p-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Berlin\, Germany\; the workshop runs alongside transmediale 2027 (
 festival 26 to 31 January 2027). Open to researchers based anywhere\, insi
 de or outside the academy.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:european-ai-society-fund-building-narrative-power-2026@artificialnouvea
 u.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T221005Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: Building Narrative Power: EUR 2m funding and learning pro
 gramme
DESCRIPTION:The European AI & Society Fund is launching Building Narrative 
 Power\, a EUR 2 million funding and learning programme to counter Big Tech
 's dominance over how AI is understood\, and to help people and communitie
 s shape new ways of thinking and talking about the technology they want. T
 he Fund's strategy is to build the power of the public interest community 
 to shape the direction of AI in Europe\; narratives are central to creatin
 g the conditions for a greater say over how AI is adopted and used. THREE 
 THEMES where research shows the public already feels strongly: (1) DATA CE
 NTRES: as the EU aims to triple data centre numbers in the next five to se
 ven years\, pollution\, energy prices and compromised grid access have mob
 ilised communities across the political spectrum\; proposals should explor
 e narrative questions that move communities beyond resistance towards enga
 gement in shaping data centre expansion. (2) AI AND JOBS: 57% of the publi
 c in a recent study expressed concern about how AI will change employment\
 , more pronounced among lower-income people\, and the concern is not only 
 displacement but the quality and nature of work\, autonomy and dignity\; p
 roposals should move the debate from generalised anxiety into a mobilising
  issue that shapes the future of work. (3) YOUNG PEOPLE: beyond the stride
 nt social media ban debate\, young people encounter AI in education\, frie
 ndships and future careers\, and their wider views are rarely heard\; prop
 osals should build narratives that help young people shape the technologie
 s that will influence their lives. LEARNING PROGRAMME: grantees join a com
 munity of practice\, sharing experiences across issues and places\, suppor
 ting each other through delivery and reflecting on which strategies and st
 ories have most salience\, with findings feeding back to the Fund and othe
 r funders. ABOUT THE FUNDER: the European AI & Society Fund is a pooled fu
 nd with 18 funding partners that mobilises a public interest community in 
 Europe to fight for AI that serves people\, society and planet\; it takes 
 no EU\, government or Big Tech money. ELIGIBILITY: this call is only open 
 to organisations in Austria\, Belgium\, Bulgaria\, Croatia\, the Republic 
 of Cyprus\, Czechia\, Denmark\, Estonia\, Finland\, France\, Germany\, Gre
 ece\, Hungary\, Iceland\, Ireland\, Italy\, Latvia\, Liechtenstein\, Lithu
 ania\, Luxembourg\, Malta\, the Netherlands\, Norway\, Poland\, Portugal\,
  Romania\, Slovakia\, Slovenia\, Spain\, Sweden and Switzerland\; applicat
 ions from the UK\, the Western Balkans and other non-European countries ar
 e not accepted. Guidance for applicants and a full application-form previe
 w are published so you can prepare a draft before submitting. HOW TO APPLY
 : create an account on the Fund's application platform Optimy (link in the
  guidance documents) and submit the first-stage application before 31 Augu
 st 2026. KEY DATES: call launched 16 July 2026\; information sessions 22 J
 uly\, 29 July and 24 August 2026 (11:00 to 12:00 CET)\; first-stage deadli
 ne 31 August 2026\; unsuccessful applicants notified 16 October 2026\; sho
 rtlisted applicants invited to submit a detailed proposal on 19 October 20
 26\, due 16 November 2026\; funding decisions notified 14 December 2026\; 
 grants start February 2027. Questions to info@europeanaifund.org\; an FAQ 
 covering eligibility\, coalitions\, non-profit status and what the grant c
 an be spent on is published at the bottom of the programme page.\n\nOrgani
 sation: European AI & Society Fund\nLocation: Organisations registered in 
 Austria\, Belgium\, Bulgaria\, Croatia\, Republic of Cyprus\, Czechia\, De
 nmark\, Estonia\, Finland\, France\, Germany\, Greece\, Hungary\, Iceland\
 , Ireland\, Italy\, Latvia\, Liechtenstein\, Lithuania\, Luxembourg\, Malt
 a\, Netherlands\, Norway\, Poland\, Portugal\, Romania\, Slovakia\, Sloven
 ia\, Spain\, Sweden or Switzerland. The UK\, the Western Balkans and other
  non-European countries are not eligible for this programme.\nAward: Grant
 s of up to EUR 150\,000 from a EUR 2 million programme\, plus a place in t
 he accompanying learning programme and community of practice. Payments are
  usually 50% on grant signing\, 40% midway through the grant period subjec
 t to previous funds having been spent\, and 10% on completion. As a genera
 l rule organisations should not request more than their previous annual bu
 dget unless the need is motivated and demonstrated\; without a previous an
 nual budget\, request an amount proportional to the project. No applicatio
 n fee.\nDuration: Project grant with an accompanying learning programme\; 
 grants start February 2027\n\nMore: https://europeanaifund.org/newspublica
 tions/call-for-proposals-new-e2m-building-narrative-power-funding-and-lear
 ning-programme/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://europeanaifund.org/newspublications/call-for-proposals-new-e2m-
 building-narrative-power-funding-and-learning-programme/?utm_source=artifi
 cialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Organisations registered in Austria\, Belgium\, Bulgaria\, Croatia
 \, Republic of Cyprus\, Czechia\, Denmark\, Estonia\, Finland\, France\, G
 ermany\, Greece\, Hungary\, Iceland\, Ireland\, Italy\, Latvia\, Liechtens
 tein\, Lithuania\, Luxembourg\, Malta\, Netherlands\, Norway\, Poland\, Po
 rtugal\, Romania\, Slovakia\, Slovenia\, Spain\, Sweden or Switzerland. Th
 e UK\, the Western Balkans and other non-European countries are not eligib
 le for this programme.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:aixdesign-kb-national-library-ai-exhibition-2026@artificialnouveau.gith
 ub.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T221005Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: AIxDESIGN x KB: Open Call for Sessions\, (Art)works and P
 erformances
DESCRIPTION:AIxDESIGN and the KB (Koninklijke Bibliotheek\, the National Li
 brary of the Netherlands) are building an exhibition and public programme 
 about AI\, made from the national collection\, at the KB in The Hague. Nad
 ia Piet is Creative Lead with the AIxDESIGN community\, co-presented with 
 the KB. The premise: long before neural nets\, libraries were the machines
  we built to organise human knowledge\, and the show uses the KB's collect
 ion to look at what AI is\, where it comes from\, and how it connects to t
 he older story of how we store\, sort\, and search what we know. Part of t
 he show lives in the KB Atelier\, with talks\, workshops\, and screenings 
 alongside\, intended as AI literacy you can wander into. WHAT THEY WANT: w
 ork that engages AI in some way (critical\, creative\, hands-on\, or playf
 ul)\; bonus if it also speaks to archives\, libraries\, or how we store an
 d sort knowledge\, though you do not need to touch the KB's collection to 
 take part. You may pitch to more than one track: (A) SOMETHING FOR THE OPE
 NING NIGHT\, a performance\, talk\, screening\, tour\, ritual\, booth\, ga
 me\, or other experience for the November 2026 opening\, fee EUR 1\,500 to
  2\,000\; (B) A SESSION IN THE EVENTS PROGRAMME\, a workshop or other sess
 ion once during the three-month run\, around 2.5 to 4 hours\, fee EUR 2\,0
 00 to 2\,500\; (C) AN (ART)WORK FOR THE EXHIBITION\, existing work in any 
 format (installation\, sculpture\, zine stand\, a corner of your own) stur
 dy enough for a public area with daily traffic\, honorarium EUR 200 to 400
  including licensing and production\; (D) SOMETHING ELSE ENTIRELY\, pitch 
 it anyway. WHO: artists\, designers\, researchers\, technologists\, perfor
 mers\, musicians\, archivists\, or educators working around AI\, data\, li
 braries\, or cultural heritage\; no finished idea or polished CV needed\, 
 just a clear\, specific pitch. You can be based anywhere and they especial
 ly welcome people outside the Netherlands\; the show is in-person in The H
 ague and the fee stays the same wherever you are coming from. HOW TO APPLY
 : send a short pitch through the form (which track\, your idea in a paragr
 aph or two\, roughly what it would need\, and a link to your work)\; takes
  about five minutes\, no full application required. Deadline: end of Augus
 t 2026. Questions to hello@aixdesign.co.\n\nOrganisation: AIxDESIGN\, co-p
 resented with the Koninklijke Bibliotheek (KB)\, National Library of the N
 etherlands\nLocation: KB\, The Hague\, Netherlands (in-person exhibition a
 nd programme)\; pitches welcome from people based anywhere\nAward: Fees by
  track: opening-night experience EUR 1\,500 to 2\,000\; a session in the e
 vents programme EUR 2\,000 to 2\,500\; an existing (art)work for the exhib
 ition EUR 200 to 400 honorarium (including licensing and production). The 
 fee stays the same wherever you are based.\nDuration: Exhibition runs Nove
 mber 2026 to February 2027\; opening event November 2026\n\nMore: https://
 nadiapiet.notion.site/Open-Call-AIxDESIGN-x-KB-Sessions-Art-works-More-38f
 37d0038338102b128c57cbeb16d2d?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_me
 dium=referral
URL:https://nadiapiet.notion.site/Open-Call-AIxDESIGN-x-KB-Sessions-Art-wor
 ks-More-38f37d0038338102b128c57cbeb16d2d?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrant
 desk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:KB\, The Hague\, Netherlands (in-person exhibition and programme)\
 ; pitches welcome from people based anywhere
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ddc-trygfonden-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T221005Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Digital Democracy Centre x TrygFonden Fellowship Program 
 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Digital Democracy Centre (DDC) at the University of Souther
 n Denmark\, with support from TrygFonden\, is calling for applications for
  its 2027 fellowship cohort. The program fosters collaboration between res
 earchers\, journalists\, and policymakers exploring the impact of digital 
 technology and AI on media\, politics\, and democracy. Fellows collaborate
  with a self-selected DDC mentor to generate new knowledge\, contribute to
  public debate\, develop networks\, and produce tangible outreach outputs 
 such as policy briefs\, educational packages\, and talks. THREE FELLOWSHIP
  TYPES: (1) academic fellows researching digital technologies and democrac
 y\; (2) journalist fellows covering digital technology and democracy\; (3)
  policy fellows contributing to the regulatory debate. Target group: resea
 rchers\, journalism graduates\, and policymakers in research/educational i
 nstitutions\, media organizations\, ministries\, NGOs\, etc. PRIORITIES: p
 rojects with clear relevance to Denmark or Danish society (including compa
 rative/international projects yielding insights relevant to a Danish conte
 xt)\; in 2027\, projects related to DDC's Hybrid Warfare Initiative (socie
 tal resilience to new forms of international conflict\, with a focus on di
 gital technologies and AI) are also prioritized. EXPECTATIONS: applicants 
 must secure confirmation of mentorship from their desired DDC mentor BEFOR
 E applying (applications without a confirmed mentor are automatically reje
 cted\; PhD students and research assistants are generally not eligible as 
 mentors\, and Sten Schaumburg-Muller is unavailable as a mentor for the 20
 27 call)\; applicants must also coordinate support from their current supe
 rvisors/managers. Fellows engage in DDC activities\, seminars/workshops\, 
 and public outreach\, and submit a short report within two months of finis
 hing. APPLICATION (in English): preliminary project title\, fellowship typ
 e\, brief project description (max 1000 chars)\, detailed description and 
 timeline (max 2400 chars\, including duration\, confirmed mentor\, and rem
 ote/in-office work plan)\, description of activities and collaboration wit
 h external practice partners (max 1000 chars)\, expected product/impact/ou
 tput (max 500 chars)\, a CV (max 5 pages)\, and an overview of the posts f
 or which financial support is sought. Submit the application form (PDF or 
 Word) plus CV as PDFs to ddc@sdu.dk with subject line 'DDC / TrygFonden Fe
 llowship application'. Deadline 1 September 2026\; decisions communicated 
 no later than 1 October 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Digital Democracy Centre (U
 niversity of Southern Denmark)\, with support from TrygFonden\nLocation: O
 dense\, Denmark (University of Southern Denmark). Flexible mix of in-offic
 e stays at the DDC in Odense and remote periods\; stays planned to the Dan
 ish working calendar.\nAward: Either a salary subsidy or a stipend to help
  with travel and/or housing\; specific benefits (financial support\, acces
 s to DDC research resources\, flexible working spaces\, speaker series\, n
 etworks) vary by agreement between the fellow\, DDC\, and partnering organ
 izations. Applicants describe the posts they seek support for (flights\, h
 ousing\, research costs\, salary compensation\, stipend\, etc.).\nDuration
 : Flexible\; typically 2-6 months (minimum two months\, can include shorte
 r DDC stays and remote periods)\, longer in exceptional cases. Fellowships
  start anywhere in 2027.\n\nMore: https://www.sdu.dk/en/forskning/forsknin
 gsenheder/samf/digitaldemocracycentre/news+folder/ddcxtrygfonden-fellowshi
 p-call-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.sdu.dk/en/forskning/forskningsenheder/samf/digitaldemocracy
 centre/news+folder/ddcxtrygfonden-fellowship-call-2027?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Odense\, Denmark (University of Southern Denmark). Flexible mix of
  in-office stays at the DDC in Odense and remote periods\; stays planned t
 o the Danish working calendar.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:musikfonds-stip-6-ai-scholarship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T221005Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260630
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260701
SUMMARY:Deadline: Musikfonds: STIP-6 AI Scholarship Programme 2027 (Germany
 )
DESCRIPTION:STIP-6 is a Musikfonds scholarship programme supporting profess
 ional musicians who work on AI-based composition and who reflect criticall
 y and societally on the role of artificial intelligence in music. Ten scho
 larships of EUR 30\,000 each are awarded for the 2027 working year. Open t
 o Germany-based professional freelance musicians across genres\; no comput
 er-science or technical background is required. Deadline 30 June 2026\, 18
 :00 CET. Details and application via the Musikfonds funding programmes pag
 e (apply at musikfonds-antrag.de). NOTE: this entry was sourced from resea
 rch and the funder's pages may update\; confirm current figures and deadli
 ne before applying.\n\nOrganisation: musikfonds e.V.\nLocation: Germany. O
 pen to Germany-based professional freelance musicians\; no computer-scienc
 e background required.\nAward: EUR 30\,000 lump sum per scholarship\, paid
  in twelve monthly installments across January to December 2027. Ten schol
 arships available. No application fee.\nDuration: Twelve months (January t
 o December 2027).\n\nMore: https://www.musikfonds.de/foerderprogramme?utm_
 source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.musikfonds.de/foerderprogramme?utm_source=artificialnouveau
 grantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Germany. Open to Germany-based professional freelance musicians\; 
 no computer-science background required.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:prototype-fund-switzerland-responsible-sustainable-ai-2026-27@artificia
 lnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T221005Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260907
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260908
SUMMARY:Deadline: Prototype Fund Switzerland 2026-27: Responsible and Susta
 inable AI
DESCRIPTION:Since 2020 the Prototype Fund Switzerland has strengthened the 
 common good with open-source\, public-interest technology\, supporting int
 erdisciplinary teams to develop and test early-stage projects that show ho
 w open source\, data and digital technologies such as AI can create real s
 ocietal value. The 2026-2027 edition focuses on Responsible and Sustainabl
 e AI: building the path toward a more responsible and sustainable AI\, tec
 h and digitalization landscape\, open source and beyond. THIS YEAR'S FOCUS
 : projects that reduce resource use\, energy consumption or emissions\; st
 rengthen accountability\, transparency and governance\; and contribute to 
 resilient and trustworthy digital systems\, including sustainable digitali
 zation and digital sufficiency\, public-interest AI and data infrastructur
 es\, and tools for governance\, oversight or digital sovereignty. PUBLIC I
 NTEREST TECH areas span sustainability and climate\, participation and civ
 ic tech\, education and literacy\, health and inclusion\, and open knowled
 ge and data. WHO THEY FUND: individuals and small teams open to open sourc
 e\, AI and iterative experimentation\, eager to combine technical capabili
 ty with societal and political awareness\, willing to share learnings\, mo
 tivated to work on pressing societal problems through technology\, with an
  early-stage but well-thought-out concept\, and eligible to work in Switze
 rland. SUPPORT: up to CHF 50\,000 in funding\, a 4-month prototyping phase
 \, coaching\, mentoring and workshops\, and access to a strong network acr
 oss tech\, policy and society. Teams are strongly encouraged (though not r
 equired) to publish code\, data or components under open-source licenses a
 nd to document their approach. TIMELINE: applications 21 July to 7 Septemb
 er 2026\; rolling jury selection August-September 2026\; prototyping mid-O
 ctober 2026 to mid-February 2027\; outputs and learnings November 2026 to 
 July 2027. Questions: info@prototypefund.ch.\n\nOrganisation: Prototype Fu
 nd Switzerland (Opendata.ch)\nLocation: Switzerland\; applicants must be e
 ligible to work in Switzerland.\nAward: Up to CHF 50\,000 per project\, pl
 us coaching\, mentoring and workshops and access to a tech/policy/society 
 network. No application fee.\nDuration: 4-month prototyping phase (mid-Oct
 ober 2026 to mid-February 2027)\n\nMore: https://prototypefund.opendata.ch
 /en/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://prototypefund.opendata.ch/en/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrant
 desk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Switzerland\; applicants must be eligible to work in Switzerland.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:we-are-human-festival-call-for-films-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T221005Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: We Are Human Festival 2026: Call for Films (Paris)
DESCRIPTION:Call for short films (1-10 minutes) involving human/AI hybrid c
 reation\, from the We Are Human Foundation in Paris. Three cash awards: Gr
 and Prix EUR 5\,000\, Best Screenplay EUR 3\,000\, and an Ethics Award EUR
  2\,000. ELIGIBILITY: international\, applicants 18+\; films must have bee
 n completed after 1 June 2025 and involve human/AI hybrid creation\; a man
 datory Ethics Notebook accompanies each submission. No submission fee. Fes
 tival screening at Forum des images\, Paris\, on 24 November 2026. Apply v
 ia FilmFreeway\; deadline 30 September 2026.\n\nOrganisation: We Are Human
  Foundation\nLocation: Paris\, France (Forum des images screening 24 Novem
 ber 2026)\nAward: Grand Prix EUR 5\,000\; Best Screenplay EUR 3\,000\; Eth
 ics Award EUR 2\,000. No submission fee.\nDuration: Annual short-film fest
 ival (screening 24 November 2026)\n\nMore: https://wearehuman.foundation/e
 n/call-for-films/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referra
 l
URL:https://wearehuman.foundation/en/call-for-films/?utm_source=artificialn
 ouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Paris\, France (Forum des images screening 24 November 2026)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:hibrides-biennial-artistic-research-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T221005Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260907
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260908
SUMMARY:Deadline: Híbrides: Small Embodied Data - Call for Contributions (
 2nd Edition)
DESCRIPTION:Híbrides is a biennial of artistic research co-organised by th
 e Axolot.cat collective and the DARTS (Design\, Art\, Technology and Socie
 ty) research group at the UOC in Barcelona. The second edition\, "Small Em
 bodied Data\," centres on the concept of classification -- approached thro
 ugh machine learning\, data science\, algorithmic systems\, and questions 
 of who designs classifiers\, under what ethical codes\, and to what end. T
 he biennial brings classification into dialogue with its previous themes o
 f Small Data (community-defined\, intentional data collection and analysis
 ) and Embodiment (bodily experience as a foundation of situated\, sensory 
 knowledge). Both academic and non-academic contributions are welcome. FORM
 ATS ACCEPTED: Oral Presentations (20 minutes\; artistic practice\, artisti
 c research\, or academic research projects\; may be video-recorded for a p
 ublic archive and developed into short papers)\; Performances (20-30 minut
 es\; must engage with the physical body\, stage\, and technology\; complet
 e works\, including previously presented work\; takes place in a multipurp
 ose space with technical support). SUBMISSION REQUIREMENTS: online form wi
 th abstract (max 1\,000 characters)\, professional biography (max 1\,000 c
 haracters)\, and link to representative materials (3-5 images\; video and 
 technical rider required for performances). HONORARIA: oral presentations 
 €100\; performances €400\; invoice required in both cases. Participant
 s must follow the event's code of conduct. Event runs 26-28 November 2026 
 at UOC Poblenou campus and Convent de Sant Agustí\, Barcelona. Deadline: 
 7 September 2026. Submit at https://hibrides.axolot.cat/opencall.html.\n\n
 Organisation: Axolot.cat collective and DARTS research group\, Universitat
  Oberta de Catalunya (UOC)\nLocation: Barcelona\, Spain\; UOC Poblenou cam
 pus and Convent de Sant Agustí. In-person attendance required.\nAward: Or
 al presentations: €100 honorarium (invoice required). Performances: €4
 00 honorarium (invoice required). No submission fee.\nDuration: Festival r
 uns 26-28 November 2026.\n\nMore: https://hibrides.axolot.cat/opencall.htm
 l?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://hibrides.axolot.cat/opencall.html?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Barcelona\, Spain\; UOC Poblenou campus and Convent de Sant Agust
 í. In-person attendance required.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:horizon-europe-cl2-2026-heritage-ai-creative@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20260827T221005Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260923
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260924
SUMMARY:Deadline: Horizon Europe Cluster 2: Culture\, Creativity and Inclus
 ive Society (HORIZON-CL2-2026-01-HERITAGE) — incl. AI and Creative Conte
 nt topic
DESCRIPTION:Horizon Europe Cluster 2 funds research on culture\, creativity
 \, and inclusive society. The 2026 call (HORIZON-CL2-2026-01-HERITAGE\, la
 unched May 12\, 2026) covers 7 topics with an indicative budget of €85.5
  million. Topic HERITAGE-04 is explicitly titled 'Towards a fair and trans
 parent market for cultural and creative content in the era of generative A
 I\,' covering: impacts of generative AI on creative industries employment\
 ; upskilling needs for creative workers\; policy and regulatory frameworks
 \; fair remuneration and transparency. Highly relevant for research into A
 I and cultural/creative industries\, artists' rights in the AI era\, and s
 ociotechnical studies of AI and culture. ELIGIBILITY: consortium-based app
 lications (minimum 3 organisations from 3 different EU/associated countrie
 s are typical for Horizon RIA grants\; check call conditions on the Fundin
 g & Tenders portal for specific requirements). Deadline: 23 September 2026
 . Apply via the EU Funding & Tenders portal (ec.europa.eu/info/funding-ten
 ders).\n\nOrganisation: European Research Executive Agency (REA) / Europea
 n Commission\, Horizon Europe\nLocation: European Union and associated cou
 ntries. Consortium-based\; typically requires partners from multiple EU/Ho
 rizon member states.\nAward: Indicative total budget €85.5 million acros
 s 7 topics (2026 call). Individual project amounts vary by topic. Up to 10
 0% funding rate for non-profit research organizations. No application fee.
 \nDuration: Typically 3-4 years\; varies by topic.\n\nMore: https://rea.ec
 .europa.eu/funding-and-grants/horizon-europe-cluster-2-culture-creativity-
 and-inclusive-society/european-cultural-heritage-and-cultural-and-creative
 -industries_en?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://rea.ec.europa.eu/funding-and-grants/horizon-europe-cluster-2-cu
 lture-creativity-and-inclusive-society/european-cultural-heritage-and-cult
 ural-and-creative-industries_en?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_
 medium=referral
LOCATION:European Union and associated countries. Consortium-based\; typica
 lly requires partners from multiple EU/Horizon member states.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:movida-labs-dance-technology-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T221005Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260803
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260804
SUMMARY:Deadline: MOVIDA: Labs on Dance and Technology 2026/27
DESCRIPTION:MOVIDA is a Creative Europe co-funded consortium pairing academ
 ic institutions with dance centres across four European cities. Sixteen du
 os\, each made up of one dancer and one creative technologist\, are select
 ed across four themed labs: AI and accessibility in Bucharest\, AI and AR/
 VR in Dusseldorf\, AI and robotics in Lisbon\, and AI and mental health in
  Tallinn. Applicants are assessed on demonstrated capability in integratin
 g technology with live performance\, computational creativity\, and audien
 ce interaction. Participants selected for the labs receive fast-track cons
 ideration for the MOVIDA residencies running in 2027 and 2028.\n\nOrganisa
 tion: MOVIDA (Movement and Virtual Intelligence for the Development and Ac
 cessibility of Artists and Audiences)\nLocation: Four labs across Europe: 
 Bucharest (Romania)\, Dusseldorf (Germany)\, Lisbon and Montemor-o-Novo (P
 ortugal)\, and Tallinn (Estonia). Applications are accepted only from duos
  of one dancer and one creative technologist\; you cannot apply solo. Both
  members must reside in an EU or Creative Europe eligible country\, so Net
 herlands-based applicants qualify.\nAward: EUR 1\,200 gross per artist (EU
 R 2\,400 per duo)\, plus travel and accommodation covered\, plus a product
 ion budget of up to EUR 500 per duo. Accessibility and personal-assistance
  costs are covered by separate agreement. No application fee.\nDuration: N
 ine days per lab\, scheduled between 3 December 2026 and 11 February 2027\
 n\nMore: https://movida.dance/news/call-labs/?utm_source=artificialnouveau
 grantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://movida.dance/news/call-labs/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantd
 esk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Four labs across Europe: Bucharest (Romania)\, Dusseldorf (Germany
 )\, Lisbon and Montemor-o-Novo (Portugal)\, and Tallinn (Estonia). Applica
 tions are accepted only from duos of one dancer and one creative technolog
 ist\; you cannot apply solo. Both members must reside in an EU or Creative
  Europe eligible country\, so Netherlands-based applicants qualify.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:waag-trust-me-im-an-airtist-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T221005Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: Trust me\, I'm an AI/rtist: Open Call for Creative Interv
 entions
DESCRIPTION:Waag Futurelab invites artists\, theatre makers\, choreographer
 s\, dancers\, game designers and other creative practitioners to engage di
 rectly with seven experimental generative AI Enablers being co-created wit
 h technologists and dance\, theatre and gaming professionals. The call is 
 an invitation into the development process: selected artists get access to
  the technologies\, datasets\, interfaces and assumptions that shape these
  systems while they are still being built. Rather than testing whether the
  systems work\, artists are asked to interrogate why they work the way the
 y do\, who they are designed for\, what forms of creativity they enable or
  constrain\, and what assumptions about culture\, intelligence\, authorshi
 p\, labour\, bodies\, audiences and society are embedded in them. Waag is 
 looking for individuals and collectives willing to move beyond adoption in
 to investigation\, and welcomes proposals that are speculative\, critical\
 , poetic\, provocative\, humorous\, performative\, constructive or disrupt
 ive\, including work that challenges\, misuses\, reimagines\, hacks\, subv
 erts or plays with the tools\, exposes hidden biases\, questions environme
 ntal costs\, or imagines alternative technological futures. THE SEVEN AI E
 NABLERS\, each requiring an on-site visit to its technology partner: the G
 eneric Procedural Content Generation Enabler (text-to-3D assets and avatar
 s\, CERTH\, Greece)\; the Data-Driven Cultural Content Adaptation Enabler 
 (audience-aware creative content ideation with ranked candidate ideas\, CE
 RTH\, Greece)\; the Cross-Domain Audience Engagement Analytics Enabler (tu
 rning heterogeneous cultural participation data into interpretable engagem
 ent signals\, The Audience Agency\, Warrington\, UK)\; the Dance Style Ana
 lysis and Generation Enabler (dance video into virtual 3D choreography\, d
 issected into body\, effort\, shape and space components\, Centre of Excel
 lence for Research & Innovation\, Cyprus)\; the Immersive Design Integrati
 on Enabler for Dance & Theatre (NLP-driven XR environments with speech-to-
 3D scene editing\, Cyprus)\; the Music-to-Movement Translation Enabler (au
 dio into a time-aligned feature set spanning genre classification\, emotio
 nal valence-arousal analysis\, rhythm extraction and latent-space projecti
 on\, feeding motion generation\, spatial audio and visual choreography\, A
 ristotle University of Thessaloniki\, Greece)\; and the Adaptive Narrative
  Enabler for Games & Theatre (an evolutionary AI trained on creator-writte
 n story elements that generates infinite story variations\, explicitly rej
 ecting LLMs because of their dependence on copyrighted or public-domain co
 rpora that encode 19th and early 20th century sexism\, racism and imperial
 ism\, CAUSA Creation\, Austria). ELIGIBILITY: artists\, designers\, creati
 ve technologists\, theatre makers\, dancers\, choreographers and game desi
 gners\, as individuals or collectives\; applicants must hold a European VA
 T number. An info session with live demonstrations of the AI Enablers runs
  31 July 2026\, 10:00 to 11:30 CEST (meeting ID 995 7016 2341). Applicatio
 ns go through Homerun using the official Application Template\, uploaded a
 s a PDF\; read the Guide for Applicants\, which contains the full Enabler 
 descriptions\, before applying. Questions: zoenie at waag dot org. The AI 
 Enablers and this call are part of the Horizon Europe project HAMLET\, whi
 ch aims to make generative AI accessible to cultural organisations of all 
 sizes. Closes 30 September 2026\, 23:59 CEST. Free to apply.\n\nOrganisati
 on: Waag Futurelab (Horizon Europe project HAMLET)\nLocation: Europe. Enga
 ging with a specific AI Enabler includes an on-site visit to its technolog
 y partner: CERTH (Greece)\, The Audience Agency (Warrington\, UK)\, the Ce
 ntre of Excellence for Research & Innovation (Cyprus)\, Aristotle Universi
 ty of Thessaloniki (Greece)\, or CAUSA Creation (Austria). Final presentat
 ions September 2027.\nAward: EUR 20\,000 commission fee excluding VAT\, in
 clusive of artist fee\, travel\, accommodation and production costs. Appli
 cants must have a VAT number in Europe. No application fee.\nDuration: Jan
 uary to September 2027\, with final presentations in September 2027\n\nMor
 e: https://waag.org/en/article/open-call-creative-interventions-trust-me-i
 m-airtist/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://waag.org/en/article/open-call-creative-interventions-trust-me-i
 m-airtist/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Europe. Engaging with a specific AI Enabler includes an on-site vi
 sit to its technology partner: CERTH (Greece)\, The Audience Agency (Warri
 ngton\, UK)\, the Centre of Excellence for Research & Innovation (Cyprus)\
 , Aristotle University of Thessaloniki (Greece)\, or CAUSA Creation (Austr
 ia). Final presentations September 2027.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:human-rights-watch-ai-strategy-solutions-lead-2026@artificialnouveau.gi
 thub.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T221005Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: Human Rights Watch: AI Strategy and Solutions Lead (one-y
 ear fixed term)
DESCRIPTION:Human Rights Watch is hiring an AI Strategy and Solutions Lead 
 into its Operations Department to build the foundation for responsible AI 
 adoption across the organisation. The role deliberately sits between strat
 egy and execution: assessing organisational readiness\, mapping partnershi
 ps and governance\, and also prototyping and shipping working systems. THE
  WORK\, in four strands. (1) STRATEGY AND READINESS: with the CIO and the 
 Information Security and Technology (IST) leadership team\, build an AI st
 rategy covering data foundation requirements\, infrastructure gaps and org
 anisational readiness\; assess current state and shape a roadmap balancing
  quick wins against long-term capacity\; research what peer nonprofit\, hu
 manitarian and human rights organisations are doing and find collaboration
  openings. (2) FLAGSHIP SOLUTIONS: work with departments to find high-impa
 ct use cases\, translate business needs into technical approaches\, build 
 and deploy AI/ML solutions with measurable value (time saved\, efficiency\
 , better outcomes)\, using the Microsoft AI ecosystem (Copilot Studio\, Az
 ure AI\, Power Platform) among other tools\, and oversee consultants and v
 endors to extend delivery capacity. (3) ADOPTION AND CAPACITY: balance onb
 oarding staff onto AI tools against building custom solutions where they a
 mplify the organisation's strengths\; write training material pitched at d
 ifferent work areas and skill levels covering prompting and responsible us
 e\; support working groups and communities of practice\; produce playbooks
  and guides for non-technical staff. (4) GOVERNANCE: partner with IST lead
 ership on risk assessment frameworks\, align solutions with security\, inf
 rastructure and architecture requirements\, contribute to AI policy develo
 pment and its operationalisation\, set standards for evaluating proofs of 
 concept and MVPs\, and engage business units so outcomes are co-owned\; al
 l of it balanced against data quality\, security and resource constraints 
 and HRW's mission as a human rights organisation. WHO THEY WANT: a bachelo
 r's degree or equivalent work experience in computer science\, data scienc
 e\, information systems\, mathematics\, AI/ML implementation or a related 
 technical field\; a minimum of seven years' relevant experience including 
 two to three years on AI and two to three years implementing AI/ML in prod
 uction\; experience contributing to technology strategy and assessing orga
 nisational readiness for new technology\; strong Python and ML library/fra
 mework skills\; hands-on predictive modelling\, data science and statistic
 al analysis\; experience with low-code/no-code AI platforms such as Copilo
 t Studio or Power Platform\; a track record of scoping and delivering unde
 r resource constraints\; a grasp of both generative AI and traditional ML\
 ; and the ability to explain technical concepts plainly to mixed audiences
 \, engage stakeholders and lead technology adoption as an internal partner
  to business teams. PREFERRED: nonprofit\, humanitarian or human rights ex
 perience\; data privacy\, security and ethical AI knowledge\; familiarity 
 with responsible AI frameworks and governance models\; vendor and consulta
 nt management\; and a background in politically sensitive or high-risk env
 ironments. APPLY: through the HRW job portal with a cover letter and CV\, 
 preferably as PDFs\, by 30 August 2026. Only complete applications are rev
 iewed and only shortlisted candidates are contacted\; no calls or email en
 quiries. HRW states that it does not discriminate on the basis of disabili
 ty\, age\, gender identity and expression\, national origin\, race and eth
 nicity\, religious beliefs\, sexual orientation or criminal record.\n\nOrg
 anisation: Human Rights Watch (HRW)\, Operations Department\nLocation: Mus
 t be within commuting distance of an HRW office: Amsterdam\, Amman\, Berli
 n\, Brussels\, London\, Stockholm\, New York\, Washington DC\, Miami\, Sao
  Paulo\, Toronto or Chicago. Reports to the Chief Information Officer in B
 erlin. No relocation support and no work-authorisation sponsorship.\nAward
 : USD 102\,000 to 112\,000 plus employer-paid benefits if based in the US\
 ; ranges outside the US are set by local market conditions. No application
  fee.\nDuration: One year fixed-term\, full time. Application deadline 30 
 August 2026.\n\nMore: https://job-boards.greenhouse.io/humanrightswatch/jo
 bs/8350654002?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://job-boards.greenhouse.io/humanrightswatch/jobs/8350654002?utm_s
 ource=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Must be within commuting distance of an HRW office: Amsterdam\, Am
 man\, Berlin\, Brussels\, London\, Stockholm\, New York\, Washington DC\, 
 Miami\, Sao Paulo\, Toronto or Chicago. Reports to the Chief Information O
 fficer in Berlin. No relocation support and no work-authorisation sponsors
 hip.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:les-antipodes-numeric-2026-the-word@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T221005Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260908
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260909
SUMMARY:Deadline: Numeric 2026: The Word (Les antipodes Research and Creati
 on Residency)
DESCRIPTION:Two research and creation residency grants in Agullana\, Catalo
 nia\, for artists\, thinkers and scientists of any branch (formal\, natura
 l\, social\, applied) working at the intersection of art\, culture\, scien
 ce and thought. THIS YEAR'S THEME\, The Word\, is squarely about large lan
 guage models. The framework moves from the word as creative principle acro
 ss traditions (the Judeo-Christian creating God\, the Quran as uncreated w
 ord\, the Sanskrit Om and the Vedas\, mantras that summon rather than desc
 ribe) to science relocating that intuition in the cell (Sagan's four-lette
 r alphabet of DNA as a material text\, Margulis countering that the langua
 ge of life is chemistry\, Witzany holding that all cell interactions are s
 ign-mediated)\, and then to LLMs\, which for the first time detach the wor
 d from the flesh and generate text through a probabilistic architecture tr
 ained on human language. The call asks what LLMs reveal about authorship\,
  originality and meaning when a system generates text that moves\, persuad
 es or deceives without experience or intention\, and whether we can still 
 say the models operate exclusively on human experience once they ingest th
 eir own output. It explicitly wants work that blurs comprehension and simu
 lation\, authorship and recombination\, and it names antecedents worth kno
 wing before applying: Ramon Llull's Ars Magna\, Joan Brossa's Sextina cibe
 rnetica\, Oulipo constraint writing\, oracles and divination\, oral-formul
 aic transmission\, translation as probabilistic approximation\, exquisite 
 corpses\, Burroughs's cut-ups\, automatic writing\, musical improvisation 
 and scientific simulation. Les antipodes asks for engagement 'beyond the i
 nstrumental use of the tools' and away from utopian and dystopian framings
 . WHAT YOU GET: spacious\, luminous\, independent work spaces plus a share
 d terrace in the centre of the village\, spaces for meeting\, dialogue and
  public presentation\, introductions to local\, academic\, artistic and pr
 ofessional contacts on request\, and support organising any activities tha
 t arise. The residency ends with an informal presentation\, and results ma
 y feed into the publications or public programme of Les antipodes. IMPORTA
 NTLY\, this is not an acquisition prize and involves no transfer of rights
 : authorship and intellectual property stay with the creators\, and select
 ion implies no acquisition of the work. Credits on resulting work must ack
 nowledge Numeric 2026 and its collaborators. WHO CAN APPLY: open to everyo
 ne of legal age\, any discipline or origin\, individually or as a collecti
 ve\, with the stipend shared among collective members. Applications must b
 e in Catalan or English whatever the working language of the project\, sub
 mitted through the online form with an optional PDF of up to 5 pages and 1
 0 MB. One application per person or collective. EVALUATION\, in order of w
 eight: singularity and innovation\; relevance to this year's LLM framework
 \; and interaction and transfer of value\, meaning relations with the soci
 al and geographical environment of Agullana and the Emporda and a plan for
  presenting the outcome in editorial\, on-site\, performative\, sound\, au
 diovisual or other form. The call may be declared void if no proposal reac
 hes the required level. PRACTICAL: residents must hold their own health co
 ver (EHIC for EU citizens\, or valid travel or health insurance for Spain 
 otherwise) and are responsible for their own equipment. Supported by the G
 eneralitat de Catalunya\, Agullana and Figueres town councils\, and Accion
  Cultural Espanola through the PICE national residencies programme. Terms 
 are published in Arabic\, Catalan\, English\, Spanish\, French and Chinese
 .\n\nOrganisation: Les antipodes\, with Bolit Centre d'Art Contemporani Gi
 rona and Museu de l'Emporda Figueres\nLocation: Agullana\, Alt Emporda\, C
 atalonia\, Spain. A village of 900 people\, 20 km from Figueres and the Vi
 lafant high-speed rail station\, 40 km from Perpignan\, 60 km from Girona\
 , 160 km from Barcelona.\nAward: Two grants. Each provides a stipend of EU
 R 1\,000 as taxable base (taxes and withholdings apply)\, round-trip trave
 l within the European Union\, and accommodation and meals for the four wee
 ks in Agullana. Travel and meals cover the resident only\, though the shar
 ed apartment can host immediate family by arrangement. Numeric also provid
 es the resources specified in the application and agreed during preparatio
 n. No application fee. Note that if a selected candidate lacks the profess
 ional registration needed to receive the stipend and payment has to be arr
 anged through Les antipodes\, the resulting costs are deducted from the st
 ipend.\nDuration: Four weeks\, 2 to 30 November 2026\, with both residenci
 es running simultaneously. Preceded by one month of preparation and follow
 ed by a two-month closing period. Applications 23 July to 8 September 2026
 \; decision announced 22 September 2026.\n\nMore: https://lesantipodes.com
 /numeric/2026_bases_en.html?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
URL:https://lesantipodes.com/numeric/2026_bases_en.html?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Agullana\, Alt Emporda\, Catalonia\, Spain. A village of 900 peopl
 e\, 20 km from Figueres and the Vilafant high-speed rail station\, 40 km f
 rom Perpignan\, 60 km from Girona\, 160 km from Barcelona.
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
